Good questions. Let me address each:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mWhy is [38;5;10maudio-processor.js[0m plain JS?[22m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
AudioWorklet processors run in a separate thread (the audio rendering thread) and are loaded via [38;5;10maudioWorklet.addModule(url)[0m — which fetches and evaluates a standalone script file. It doesn't go through Vite's bundler pipeline, so it can't use TypeScript, [38;5;10mimport[0m statements, or any module resolution. It has to be a self-contained vanilla JS file.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mWhy is it in [38;5;10mclient/public/[0m?[22m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Because [38;5;10maddModule()[0m takes a URL path, not a module import. Vite serves files from [38;5;10mpublic/[0m as static assets at the root path — so [38;5;10mpublic/audio-processor.js[0m becomes available at [38;5;10m/audio-processor.js[0m at runtime. That's exactly what the hook does:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mtypescript
[0m[38;5;10mawait ctx.audioWorklet.addModule("/audio-processor.js");
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
If it were anywhere in [38;5;10msrc/[0m, Vite would try to bundle it, which breaks the AudioWorklet loading mechanism.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mReplacing the for loop in [38;5;10mresample[0m[22m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The linear interpolation resampling needs per-sample index math ([38;5;10msrcIdx[0m, fractional blending between two source samples), so there's no built-in array method that does this cleanly. A [38;5;10mfor[0m loop is the standard approach for this kind of DSP work — you'll see the same pattern in Web Audio libraries and even in the WAV encoder in [38;5;10msoundUtils.ts[0m. Replacing it with something like [38;5;10m.map()[0m would just add overhead (creating an intermediate array, callback per sample) with no readability gain for a numeric computation like this.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
That said, if the concern is about the loop being verbose, I could extract it into a one-liner using [38;5;10mFloat32Array.from()[0m:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mtypescript
[0m[38;5;10mfunction resample(samples: Float32Array, fromRate: number, toRate: number): Float32Array {
  const ratio = fromRate / toRate;
  const len = Math.round(samples.length / ratio);
  return Float32Array.from({ length: len }, (_, i) => {
    const s = i * ratio;
    const lo = Math.floor(s);
    return samples[lo] * (1 - (s - lo)) + samples[Math.min(lo + 1, samples.length - 1)] * (s - lo);
  });
}
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
But it's functionally identical and slightly slower due to the callback overhead on what could be 64k+ samples. I'd leave the [38;5;10mfor[0m loop as-is for a hot audio path. Want me to change it anyway?[0m[0m
