There are a few options, each with tradeoffs:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mOption 1: Write it in TS in [38;5;10msrc/[0m, build it separately[22m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
You'd add a second Vite entry point (or a small build script) that compiles the TS file into a standalone JS file output to [38;5;10mpublic/[0m. This works but adds build complexity — you need to make sure the worklet JS is built before the main app serves it. Libraries like [38;5;10mvite-plugin-audio-worklet[0m exist for this, but it's another dependency.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mOption 2: Write the logic in TS, keep the processor shell in JS[22m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
This is the pragmatic approach — extract the buffering logic into a testable TS module, and keep the thin AudioWorklet glue in JS since that's the part that [3mmust[23m be a standalone script:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10msrc/hooks/useAudioCapture/audio-buffer.ts       ← testable TS logic
src/hooks/useAudioCapture/audio-buffer.test.ts   ← tests
public/audio-processor.js                        ← thin shell, just calls buffer logic
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The problem: the worklet can't import from [38;5;10msrc/[0m at runtime. So the JS file would still duplicate the buffering logic. You'd be testing a TS copy that mirrors the JS, which is better than nothing but not a true integration test.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mOption 3: Inline the worklet as a Blob URL[22m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
This is the cleanest way to make it "TypeScript" and testable:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mtypescript
[0m[38;5;10mconst processorCode = `
  class AudioCaptureProcessor extends AudioWorkletProcessor {
    // ... same code but as a string
