  registerProcessor("audio-capture-processor", AudioCaptureProcessor);
`;

const blob = new Blob([processorCode], { type: "application/javascript" });
const url = URL.createObjectURL(blob);
await ctx.audioWorklet.addModule(url);
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
This eliminates the [38;5;10mpublic/[0m file entirely, but the processor code is still a string literal — no TS type checking inside it.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mMy recommendation[22m: The processor is 40 lines of straightforward buffer-and-post logic. The real complexity lives in the hook and WAV encoder, which are already in TS and tested. AudioWorklet processors are inherently a browser API boundary — they run in a separate thread with no module system. Trying to force TS onto them adds build complexity without meaningful safety gains for such a small file.[0m[0m
